\section{The nugget}{

A squared exponential kernel is near singular, so a nugget on its diagonal
keeps it factorable. \code{frm()} adds \code{1e-6 * sdgp^2}, \code{frm_sample()} in
frmtmb.sample adds \code{1e-12 * sdgp^2} when it non-centers the field, and brms
adds an absolute \code{1e-12}. \code{frm()}'s centered field usually does not converge
below 1e-6. A relative nugget also does not depend on the units of y. brms's
does: at y times 1000 its kernel does not factor at frm()'s estimates (its
run reads Rhat 3.77), and at y times 1e-3 its nugget floors \code{sigma}.

At ordinary noise the two models have one maximum: brms's model, read at
frm()'s maximum, loses at most 3e-9 nats on three designs of 200 rows.
\code{frm()} and brms differ when the nugget reaches the noise at a position:
\itemize{
\item With one row per position and an estimated \code{sigma}, the nugget's variance
comes off \code{sigma^2}: \code{sigma_frm^2 = sigma_brms^2 - 1e-6 * sdgp^2}. Below
\code{sigma = 1e-3 * sdgp} the fit collapses to a \code{sigma} near 0, and \code{sdgp}
moves too.
\item Where positions repeat, or \code{sigma} is known through \code{se()}, or the family
has no \code{sigma} (a Poisson at a large mean, a binomial at very many trials),
\code{sdgp} and the length scale read low. A binomial at 1e7 trials reads \code{sdgp}
0.73 for 2.87; at 1e5 the two differ by about what another seed does.
}

It depends on the range of the field against the noise, not on the size of
the noise. \code{y ~ gp(x)} with x on 0 to 10, y a curve \code{x^2} and a true \code{sigma}
of 0.3 reads \code{sigma} 1e-4 where brms reads 0.34.
}

\section{The warning}{

\code{frm()} warns with class \code{frmtmb_gp_nugget}, on top of \code{frmtmb_warning}, when
the 90th percentile over the positions of the nugget's variance over the
noise at a position reaches \code{sqrt(2 / m)} for \code{m} positions, which is about
one standard error of \code{sigma}. The noise at a position is read from the
likelihood's curvature, so the rule is the same for every family and route.
\code{frm()} does not check an \verb{importance =} or \verb{integrate =} fit. \code{frm_sample()}
draws with its own nugget and gives no such warning.
}

\section{What to do about it}{

\code{frm_sample()} reads the model at its smaller nugget, on two routes.
\code{laplace = TRUE} integrates the field out, and it mixes fast at low noise,
for the outer parameters only (\code{sigma}, \code{sdgp}, the length scales): it refuses
\code{posterior_epred()}, \code{posterior_linpred()}, \code{posterior_predict()},
\code{fitted()}, \code{predict()}, \code{log_lik()}, \code{loo()} and \code{conditional_effects()}.
The drawn route, the default, is the one for the field and for predictions,
and at low noise it needs long chains (at sigma 1e-3 and 15 rows, Rhat 1.03
on \code{sigma} after an hour).
\code{gp(x, k = )} has no nugget and reaches brms's numbers when \code{k} is large
enough for the field. Too small a \code{k} is a different failure, with no
warning and with \code{convergence} 0: \code{sin(8 * x)} on 0 to 6 with noise 1e-3 at
\code{k = 30} has a basis that stops
at frequency 6.5, so it reads \code{sigma} 720 times high; \code{k = 60} reads it.
Compare several \code{k}. A smooth trend written as fixed effects also lowers what
the field has to carry.
}

\section{The order of the field}{

\code{frm_sample()}'s \code{b[i]} columns and the levels of \code{ranef()} of an exact
term hold the field at the sorted distinct positions, where brms keeps first
appearance. They are not in the order of the rows of the data. Read a field
at the data through \code{posterior_epred()} or \code{frm_linpred()}, which are right
row by row.
}

